1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,088
A CI job restores its workspace from a
cache, and git status reports every tracked

2
00:00:10,128 --> 00:00:15,784
file as modified. A second job copies the
repository into a container with COPY and

3
00:00:15,824 --> 00:00:20,794
sees the same thing. An engineer
restores a laptop from backup: the same.

4
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:22,373
Nobody edited anything.

5
00:00:22,713 --> 00:00:28,231
And one more, from the first page of Chapter
3: a laptop lost power during a commit,

6
00:00:28,271 --> 00:00:33,525
and Git now says fatal: index file corrupt.
Your CTO asks: what did we lose, the

7
00:00:33,565 --> 00:00:38,356
history, the work in progress, or
nothing? Pick one, and say it out loud.

8
00:00:43,196 --> 00:00:48,285
Both questions are about one file, .git/index,
and both have a one-command answer

9
00:00:48,325 --> 00:00:52,084
once you know what that file
contains. Hold on to your pick.

10
00:00:52,124 --> 00:00:53,033
We'll check it.

11
00:00:54,873 --> 00:00:58,547
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:58,587 --> 00:01:03,661
You've used the index, also called the
staging area, as a concept since the start of

13
00:01:03,701 --> 00:01:07,679
the course: the proposed next
commit. Today it's a data structure.

14
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:12,610
You'll read its header with xxd, decode
its entries, watch its cache at work, see

15
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:16,020
its flags and its stage numbers,
and change its version.

16
00:01:16,360 --> 00:01:23,754
Two replays from labs/ch03, index-file.sh
and index-debug.sh, then the replay of Lab

17
00:01:23,794 --> 00:01:29,773
17.1 up to its checkpoint. A sixty-line
Python script in the course,

18
00:01:29,813 --> 00:01:36,057
labs/ch03/read-index.py, decodes the
file by following the manual page.

19
00:01:36,097 --> 00:01:38,004
You'll see its output.

20
00:01:38,344 --> 00:01:44,669
Labels: git ls-files is 🟢 SAFE. git
update-index --index-version 4 is 🟢: it changes

21
00:01:44,709 --> 00:01:47,889
the storage of the index, not its content.

22
00:01:49,729 --> 00:01:51,142
After this video you can:

23
00:01:51,482 --> 00:01:53,452
List the fields of an index entry.

24
00:01:53,792 --> 00:01:59,165
Explain why every file shows as modified
after a repository is copied, and repair it

25
00:01:59,205 --> 00:02:00,261
with one command.

26
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:06,522
Read flags and stage numbers in git
ls-files --debug and --stage output.

27
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:08,957
Say what the index version changes.

28
00:02:09,297 --> 00:02:13,909
Create a commit without using the index.
This last objective is practised in the

29
00:02:13,949 --> 00:02:17,456
homework exercise; the cited
section does not demonstrate it.

30
00:02:19,296 --> 00:02:24,386
Why look inside? Because the index does two
jobs, and incidents come from confusing

31
00:02:24,426 --> 00:02:24,695
them.

32
00:02:25,035 --> 00:02:30,160
In one sentence: the index is one binary
file that records, for every tracked path,

33
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,200
a mode, a blob ID, a stage number and a
copy of the file's stat data, followed by

34
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:40,177
extensions that let Git skip work. It's
at once the proposed next commit and the

35
00:02:40,217 --> 00:02:45,404
cache that makes git status fast. The stat
data is what the filesystem reports about

36
00:02:45,444 --> 00:02:47,830
a file, such as its size and its times.

37
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:51,863
The format is specified in
git help gitformat-index.

38
00:02:51,903 --> 00:02:57,820
A 12-byte header: the signature DIRC, a
version, which is 2, 3 or 4, and the number

39
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:02,988
of entries. Then the entries, sorted
by path as bytes and then by stage.

40
00:03:03,328 --> 00:03:08,783
Each entry has three parts before its
path. First, 40 bytes of stat data: change

41
00:03:08,823 --> 00:03:14,553
time and modification time with nanoseconds,
device, inode, mode, user, group, size.

42
00:03:14,593 --> 00:03:20,185
Second, the object ID. Third, 16 bits of
flags: an assume-valid bit, an "extended"

43
00:03:20,225 --> 00:03:23,688
bit, the two-bit stage, and
the length of the path.

44
00:03:23,728 --> 00:03:28,908
Version 3 adds 16 more bits, with the
skip-worktree and intent-to-add flags.

45
00:03:28,948 --> 00:03:33,236
The path follows, padded with NUL
bytes to a multiple of eight.

46
00:03:33,276 --> 00:03:38,593
Version 4 compresses each path against
the previous one and drops the padding.

47
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:44,166
After the entries come the extensions,
each a four-letter signature, a length and

48
00:03:44,206 --> 00:03:49,568
data. TREE caches the tree IDs that unchanged
parts of the index already correspond

49
00:03:49,608 --> 00:03:54,580
to. REUC keeps the stages of a resolved
conflict, so that git checkout -m can

50
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:59,853
recreate it. Others serve the split index,
the untracked cache and the filesystem

51
00:03:59,893 --> 00:04:05,256
monitor. The file ends with a checksum,
unless index.skipHash replaces it by zeros.

52
00:04:05,596 --> 00:04:10,804
Inside .git: one file, index, rewritten as
a whole by every command that changes it.

53
00:04:10,844 --> 00:04:15,990
That includes git status, which refreshes
the stat data and writes the result back.

54
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:21,337
Now the mechanism behind the hook. Git
trusts the cached stat data to skip reading

55
00:04:21,377 --> 00:04:26,445
files. If the size, the times, the inode
and the rest still match, Git doesn't open

56
00:04:26,485 --> 00:04:30,938
the file. A copy of a repository gives
every file a new inode number, the

57
00:04:30,978 --> 00:04:34,507
filesystem's own number for a
file, and a new change time.

58
00:04:34,547 --> 00:04:38,077
The content is identical, and
every cached entry is stale.

59
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:39,985
Nothing is wrong with the data.

60
00:04:40,325 --> 00:04:44,933
And the failure mode of the second question.
The index is derived data, with one

61
00:04:44,973 --> 00:04:49,291
exception: content that was staged and
never committed is named only there.

62
00:04:49,331 --> 00:04:54,172
So fatal: index file corrupt is repaired by
deleting the file and running git reset.

63
00:04:54,212 --> 00:04:58,937
History and working tree are untouched,
and only what was staged and not committed

64
00:04:58,977 --> 00:05:03,760
must be staged again. If you picked nothing,
you were close: that staged content is

65
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:05,038
the one thing to redo.

66
00:05:06,878 --> 00:05:11,766
The textbook's analogy is a packing list
for the next shipment, with an inspection

67
00:05:11,806 --> 00:05:16,513
sticker on every line saying when the
item was last checked and how big it was.

68
00:05:16,553 --> 00:05:21,320
The list becomes the commit's tree. The
stickers let the clerk skip boxes nobody

69
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:21,801
touched.

70
00:05:22,141 --> 00:05:26,493
The analogy breaks during a conflict,
when the list carries three lines for one

71
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:28,605
item: the merge base, ours and theirs.

72
00:05:28,945 --> 00:05:33,506
Keep the two halves of each line apart.
The left half, mode, ID, path, is content:

73
00:05:33,546 --> 00:05:37,882
it's the next commit. The right half,
the sticker, is a cache: it can be wrong

74
00:05:37,922 --> 00:05:42,033
without anything being lost, and it's
refreshed by reading the file again.

75
00:05:43,873 --> 00:05:48,086
Read the header first: twelve
bytes. Then one entry, opened up.

76
00:05:48,126 --> 00:05:51,798
Its first row is the sticker:
forty bytes of stat data.

77
00:05:51,838 --> 00:05:57,401
Its second row is the object ID, twenty
bytes. The third is two bytes of flags, and

78
00:05:57,441 --> 00:06:00,438
inside them the two-bit
stage. Then the path.

79
00:06:00,778 --> 00:06:04,680
Below: one entry per path, and
per stage during a conflict.

80
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:08,823
Then the tail of extensions, TREE
and REUC. Then the checksum.

81
00:06:13,163 --> 00:06:18,256
Eight tracked paths. Predict the twelve
bytes: what are the first four as text, and

82
00:06:18,296 --> 00:06:20,421
what is the last number? I'll wait.

83
00:06:25,261 --> 00:06:29,960
4449 5243 is DIRC, then
version 2, then eight entries.

84
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,191
In the listing above it, the columns
are mode, blob ID, stage and path.

85
00:06:36,231 --> 00:06:38,999
Stage 0 everywhere: no conflict.

86
00:06:39,339 --> 00:06:44,767
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git ls-files

87
00:06:44,807 --> 00:06:47,922
--stage and read the
third column. I'll wait.

88
00:06:52,762 --> 00:06:56,914
That column is the stage, and zero
on every line means no conflict.

89
00:06:57,254 --> 00:07:02,426
The decoded file: the header, eight entries,
a TREE extension, a valid checksum.

90
00:07:05,266 --> 00:07:11,137
git ls-files --debug prints the sticker:
change time, modification time, device,

91
00:07:11,177 --> 00:07:16,825
inode, user, group, size and flags. Below
it, the same numbers asked from the

92
00:07:16,865 --> 00:07:22,440
filesystem with stat. They match. That
match is what lets Git skip the file.

93
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:27,807
The content is unchanged. What does the
plumbing command git diff-files print the

94
00:07:27,847 --> 00:07:30,871
first time, and the second
time? Say it out loud.

95
00:07:35,711 --> 00:07:40,637
The first time it reports the path as
possibly changed, with an all-zero ID that

96
00:07:40,677 --> 00:07:45,231
means "look at the working tree".
Plumbing compares cached stat data only.

97
00:07:45,271 --> 00:07:50,321
Then git status reads the file, hashes it,
finds the blob the index already names,

98
00:07:50,361 --> 00:07:54,356
and writes fresh stat data back.
The second diff-files is silent.

99
00:07:54,696 --> 00:07:56,514
Now copy a whole repository.

100
00:07:56,854 --> 00:08:01,894
Every entry is stale, and no file has
changed. One refresh repairs it: git

101
00:08:01,934 --> 00:08:07,181
update-index --refresh. This is the whole
explanation of "every file shows as

102
00:08:07,221 --> 00:08:12,536
modified" after a restore from backup,
a CI cache, or a COPY into a container.

103
00:08:12,576 --> 00:08:14,321
It's never a data problem.

104
00:08:14,661 --> 00:08:15,263
The flags.

105
00:08:15,603 --> 00:08:20,507
Look at the header line when it appears.
Which field changed without anyone asking?

106
00:08:20,547 --> 00:08:21,758
Make your prediction.

107
00:08:26,598 --> 00:08:32,131
The version. Setting a version-3 flag
upgraded the file from version 2 to version 3

108
00:08:32,171 --> 00:08:36,293
on the spot. And the intent-to-add
entry names the empty blob.

109
00:08:36,333 --> 00:08:39,046
Chapter 5 explains what each flag is for.

110
00:08:39,386 --> 00:08:45,398
Stages. A quick quiz first: during a
conflict, how many entries does the one path

111
00:08:45,438 --> 00:08:49,059
config.toml have: one, two
or three? Your answer?

112
00:08:53,899 --> 00:08:59,693
Three. One path, three entries. Stage 1 is
the merge base, 2 is ours, 3 is theirs.

113
00:08:59,733 --> 00:09:04,032
The colon syntax from the rev-parse
video reads each of them.

114
00:09:04,372 --> 00:09:10,220
git add replaced the three entries by one
stage-0 entry and moved them into the REUC

115
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:12,673
extension, the resolve-undo record.

116
00:09:13,013 --> 00:09:14,044
The version field.

117
00:09:14,384 --> 00:09:18,130
Version 4 saved 38 bytes here
by sharing path prefixes.

118
00:09:18,170 --> 00:09:23,086
In a repository with a hundred thousand
paths, that saving is what makes

119
00:09:23,126 --> 00:09:25,633
feature.manyFiles worth switching on.

120
00:09:28,473 --> 00:09:31,819
The lab repeats these steps
on its own small repository.

121
00:09:31,859 --> 00:09:36,716
Notice one thing here: after git add, the
entry already names a blob that exists.

122
00:09:36,756 --> 00:09:41,795
git add wrote the object. The lab continues
through the stat cache and a conflict to

123
00:09:41,835 --> 00:09:46,753
its checkpoint. The failure scenario, a
corrupt index, and its recovery are yours.

124
00:09:48,593 --> 00:09:50,241
Five mistakes to watch for.

125
00:09:50,581 --> 00:09:54,558
Treating "everything is modified"
after a copy as changed files.

126
00:09:54,598 --> 00:09:59,830
Root cause: the copy has new inode numbers
and change times, so the cached stat data

127
00:09:59,870 --> 00:10:02,341
is stale while the content is identical.

128
00:10:02,681 --> 00:10:05,785
Treating fatal: index file
corrupt as lost history.

129
00:10:05,825 --> 00:10:10,531
Root cause: the index is derived data;
only content that was staged and never

130
00:10:10,571 --> 00:10:12,503
committed is named nowhere else.

131
00:10:12,843 --> 00:10:15,926
Reading git diff-files
output as proof of a change.

132
00:10:15,966 --> 00:10:20,764
Root cause: plumbing compares cached stat
data only and does not read the file.

133
00:10:21,104 --> 00:10:26,010
Being surprised that the index version
changed. Root cause: skip-worktree and

134
00:10:26,050 --> 00:10:30,442
intent-to-add are version-3 flags,
and setting one upgrades the file.

135
00:10:30,782 --> 00:10:36,230
Expecting one entry per path at all times.
Root cause: during a conflict a path has

136
00:10:36,270 --> 00:10:37,486
an entry per stage.

137
00:10:39,326 --> 00:10:43,849
Now, out of the lab. Three illustrative
incidents for one backend team, and the

138
00:10:43,889 --> 00:10:45,178
first command for each.

139
00:10:45,518 --> 00:10:50,185
"Everything is modified after the
restore": git update-index --refresh.

140
00:10:50,225 --> 00:10:55,622
The CI job that restores a cached workspace
now runs it, or any git status, before

141
00:10:55,662 --> 00:10:57,876
it asks whether the tree is clean.

142
00:10:58,216 --> 00:11:03,845
"git status takes a minute on the monorepo":
the stat cache already does its job.

143
00:11:03,885 --> 00:11:09,655
The next steps are core.untrackedCache,
core.fsmonitor and feature.manyFiles, which

144
00:11:09,695 --> 00:11:10,915
Chapter 26 covers.

145
00:11:11,255 --> 00:11:16,162
"fatal: index file corrupt" after the power
loss: move the file aside, so that you

146
00:11:16,202 --> 00:11:21,231
can put it back, and run git reset. History
and working tree are untouched, and only

147
00:11:21,271 --> 00:11:24,549
what was staged and not
committed must be staged again.

148
00:11:24,589 --> 00:11:28,652
That's the failure scenario of Lab
17.1, and the answer for the CTO.

149
00:11:30,492 --> 00:11:34,857
Your turn. Do Lab 17.1, "Read the
index as a data structure", in

150
00:11:34,897 --> 00:11:40,294
lab-manual/m17-index-refs-gitdir.md.
Before each step, predict the header line:

151
00:11:40,334 --> 00:11:45,938
version and entry count. Before the conflict,
predict how many entries config.toml

152
00:11:45,978 --> 00:11:51,719
will have and what each stage holds. Before
the recovery, write down what you expect

153
00:11:51,759 --> 00:11:52,614
to have lost.

154
00:11:52,954 --> 00:11:58,874
The challenge is Exercise 17.8, "An
edit that Git does not see", in

155
00:11:58,914 --> 00:12:01,631
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

156
00:12:03,471 --> 00:12:06,231
Question 74 of the CTO question bank:

157
00:12:06,571 --> 00:12:11,560
A strong answer says what the index caches
for each entry and why, names which of

158
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:16,775
those values a copy changes, and distinguishes
the command that compares cached data

159
00:12:16,815 --> 00:12:21,928
from the command that reads the file. It
gives the fix and adds, unprompted, why no

160
00:12:21,968 --> 00:12:22,984
data was at risk.

161
00:12:24,824 --> 00:12:29,850
Let's land this. One file, two jobs: the
next commit, and a cache that can be stale

162
00:12:29,890 --> 00:12:31,559
without anything being lost.

163
00:12:31,899 --> 00:12:33,559
You should now be able to say:

164
00:12:33,899 --> 00:12:37,680
The index is a header, sorted
entries, extensions and a checksum.

165
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:43,252
An entry is stat data, an object ID,
flags with a two-bit stage, and a path.

166
00:12:43,592 --> 00:12:49,046
Git trusts the stat data to skip reading
files; a copy makes it stale, and git

167
00:12:49,086 --> 00:12:51,440
update-index --refresh repairs it.

168
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:57,280
A conflict puts three entries for one path
into the index; resolving moves them into

169
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:57,609
REUC.

170
00:12:57,949 --> 00:13:01,488
Version 3 adds flags;
version 4 compresses paths.

171
00:13:03,328 --> 00:13:10,798
Read section 3.12 of Chapter 3. Do Exercise
17.1, "The index as a table", Exercise

172
00:13:10,838 --> 00:13:17,850
17.4, "The index during a conflict", and
Exercise 17.6, "A commit without the

173
00:13:17,890 --> 00:13:21,696
index", in exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

174
00:13:22,036 --> 00:13:27,558
Today you read the index byte by byte, and
you can calm a room that sees every file

175
00:13:27,598 --> 00:13:33,052
modified. Do the lab before the next video:
the reftable backend, and SHA-1 versus

176
00:13:33,092 --> 00:13:37,139
